From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000051 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@local>
Date: Tue, 12 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mips: sf2000: mark final restore assembly

The physical SF2000 reaches sf2000_ret_before_restore() for the first /init
return but never reaches the syscall, generic exception, or TLB fault markers.
That leaves an important ambiguity: the CPU may be dying while RESTORE_SOME
writes the user Status value and reloads saved registers, before the final
eret is even executed.

Write a tiny no-call marker into the retained progress header immediately
after RESTORE_SOME. The loader already dumps these reserved words on warm
boot, so the next physical log can distinguish a RESTORE_SOME failure from a
final eret/refill failure without perturbing the generated TLB refill handler.
---
 arch/mips/kernel/entry.S | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/mips/kernel/entry.S b/arch/mips/kernel/entry.S
--- a/arch/mips/kernel/entry.S
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/entry.S
@@ -128,5 +128,20 @@ restore_partial:		# restore partial frame
 	RESTORE_SOME
+#ifdef CONFIG_MIPS_SF2000
+	/*
+	 * No calls here: user registers have been restored and CP0_STATUS may
+	 * already contain the final user/ERL value. k0/k1 are not user ABI
+	 * state, and 0xa13f0010 is the retained progress header reserved area.
+	 */
+	lui	k0, 0xa13f
+	li	k1, 0x51510001
+	sw	k1, 16(k0)
+	mfc0	k1, CP0_STATUS
+	sw	k1, 20(k0)
+	MFC0	k1, CP0_EPC
+	sw	k1, 24(k0)
+	sync
+#endif
 	RESTORE_SP_AND_RET
 	.set	at
 
 work_pending:
